#Data Normalization
Normally the real world data is often broken down into pieces and stored across multiple orthogonal tables using a process called Database organization
#Primary key
Tables that share information about a single entity , need to have a primary key (that identifies the entity across the database)
#INNER JOIN /JOIN
We can use JOIN/INNER JOIN statement to combine row data across two different columns using the primary key
## QUERY
Select Column, another_column
From mytable
INNER JOIN another_table
On mytable.id = another_table.id
Where condition(s)
Order By column ASC/DESC
Limit num_limit, offset num_offset;
### Practice Example:-
1-Show sales number for each movies that did better internationally rather than domestically
 Select Title, Domestic_sales, International_sales
 From Movies
 Inner Join Boxoffice
 On Movies.id = Boxoffice.Movie_id
 Where International_sales > Domestic_sales;

2- List all the movies by their rating in descending order
 Select title, Ratings
 From Movies
 Join Boxoffice
 On Movie.id= Boxoffice.Movie_id
 Order By Ratings Desc;

#OUTER JOIN
If two tables have asymmetric data ,then we have to use Left JOIN, RIGHT JOIN, and Full JOIN
## QUERY
 Select column, another_column
 from mytable
 Left/Right/Full Join another_table
 On mytable.id= another_table.matching_id
 Where condition(s)
 Order By column ASC/DESC
 Limit num_limit Offset num_offset;
###Practice Example:-
List all buildings and distinct employees roles in each building (including empty buildings)
 Select Distinct Building, name, roles
 From Building
 LEFT JOIN employees
 ON Building.name= employees.building;
